# Loader for Scratch `.sb3` project files -- 100% Boring-authored port of the
# former `src/sb3_loader.rs` (hand-written Rust; deleted once this file was
# verified equivalent). Loads a `.sb3` zip archive (or a raw `project.json`)
# into plain structs; turning those into a real block AST/instance model is
# `boring/scratch.br`'s own linker's job (`convert_target`/`parse_variable`/
# etc. below only concern themselves with faithfully reflecting the on-disk
# JSON shape, matching the original file's own scope).
#
# Design still mirrors jscratch's `core/json` package, same as the original
# Rust file's own doc comment described.
#
# Sibling file to `scratch.br` (same directory) -- pulled in via a bare
# `use sb3_loader` there, a genuine same-project multi-file mechanism
# (undocumented in book.md's own "Modules" section, confirmed empirically
# against `boring/interpreter/`'s own multi-file layout): every top-level
# item here is spliced into the same generated Rust file `scratch.br`'s own
# code lives in, so `pub` here only matters for `tests/*.rs` (a separate
# crate) -- see each type's own comment for which ones need it.
#
# ─── Why this port was possible now, and wasn't before ──────────────────────
# The original file's own doc comment named exactly two reasons it stayed
# hand-written Rust: (1) Boring had no field-renaming for `@derive(Deserialize)`
# (fixed 2026-08-22, `boring` commit c35be74), and (2) most of its own JSON
# shape is genuinely dynamic (a variable's value can be a JSON number, string,
# or bool; a block input can be a block-id string or an inline primitive
# array; etc.) and Boring has no dynamic/untyped JSON value type. That second
# gap is closed here not by adding one to Boring, but by using a plain
# Boring-declared `@serde(untagged)` enum (`JVal` below) -- serde's own
# untagged-enum deserialization already does exactly what a hand-rolled
# `serde_json::Value` walk did in the original, and `@serde(...)` passthrough
# (already used elsewhere for `rename_all`) applies here for free. Verified
# against real `serde_json` (not just `boring run`'s interpreter, which was
# found to have its own, different -- and for this specific case, wrong --
# `fromJson`/enum-Display behavior for untagged enums; always verify this
# file's own behavior via `boring build` + `cargo test`, matching every other
# Boring-authored part of this project).
#
# ─── Deliberate deviations from the original Rust file ──────────────────────
# - No `Sb3LoadError` enum. The original's own Boring-integration section
#   already collapsed every fallible load into `Option<Sb3Project>`
#   (`load_for_boring`/`load_json_for_boring`) because "`Result<_,
#   Sb3LoadError>` isn't Boring's own `throws` machinery, and mapping an
#   arbitrary external error type through that would be its own can of worms"
#   -- since that Option-collapsing entry point is now the *only* one (no Rust
#   caller needs the rich `Result` form -- `tests/scratch_vm_fixtures.rs`'s
#   two direct callers were updated to use it too), the seven-variant error
#   enum + its `Display`/`Error` impls (the original file's own second-largest
#   section) is dropped entirely. Every parse step below just returns `T?`,
#   `nil` on any shape mismatch -- exactly as forgiving/unforgiving as the
#   original's own error *behavior* (a malformed project still fails to
#   load), just without a descriptive message to show for it.
# - `find_entry_at_root_or_one_level_deep` (the original's own prefix-tolerant
#   zip lookup, shared with `costume_rt.rs`/`sound_rt.rs`'s Rust code before
#   they too were ported to Boring) is reimplemented here using
#   `ZipArchive.byIndex`/`.len()` + `.split("/")` on each entry name, rather
#   than the original's `Iterator::find` + `str::strip_suffix` -- Boring has
#   no generic external-iterator consumption, but indexed access sidesteps
#   that entirely, and `.split("/")`'s segment count is a cleaner equivalent
#   to the original's `prefix.ends_with('/') && !prefix[..len-1].contains('/')`
#   check anyway (see `zip_entry_matches`'s own comment). This project's own
#   `boring/scratch.br` `read_zip_entry_bytes` used to call this file's
#   `Sb3Project.findZipEntryForBoring` thin Rust delegate for the exact same
#   lookup; it now inlines the same by-index search directly instead, since a
#   Boring-declared function can't cleanly take an external generic type
#   (`ZipArchive<File>`) as a parameter (see that function's own comment) --
#   the way a Rust `fn(&ZipArchive<File>, &str)` trivially could.

# `File`/`Read`/`ZipArchive`/`ZipFile` are NOT re-`use`d here even though
# this file needs all four (`read_project_json_from_zip` below) -- once
# inlined, both this file's and `scratch.br`'s own top-level items land in
# the same flat Rust scope (see this file's own header comment on `use
# sb3_loader`), and `scratch.br` already brings in the exact same four names
# for its own costume/sound extraction code. A second identical `use` in
# that same scope is a real Rust error (E0252 "name defined multiple
# times"), confirmed the hard way -- unlike a module-scoped re-import,
# Rust does not tolerate a duplicate `use` of the very same item.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dynamic JSON value
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A genuinely dynamic JSON value -- null/bool/number/string/array/object,
# shape unknown until parsed. `@serde(untagged)` (passed through verbatim to
# real `serde`, same mechanism as a struct's `@serde(rename_all = ...)`) is
# what makes this work: serde tries each variant in declaration order until
# one parses, which is exactly "guess the JSON shape" -- no hand-rolled
# parser needed. Used everywhere the original Rust file used a raw
# `serde_json::Value`: a variable/list's initial value, a block input/field's
# raw array, a monitor's `params`/`value`.
@derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)
@serde(untagged)
enum JVal:
    JNull()
    JBool(bool)
    JNum(float)
    JStr(string)
    JArr([JVal])
    JObj({string=JVal})

    # `match self.clone():` (not a bare `match self:`) throughout this enum's
    # own methods -- `req` means `&self`, and matching a reference binds its
    # payloads by reference too (`n: &f64`, not `f64`); `n as int64` then
    # fails to compile ("casting `&f64` as `i64` is invalid", a real E0606
    # from `cargo build`, not a hypothetical). Cloning first gives the match
    # owned bindings. Distinct from (and NOT fixed by) docs/self-field-loop-
    # match-borrow-bug.md in the `boring` repo -- that bug/fix was about a
    # bare *field* reference (`match some_field:`); matching bare `self`
    # itself is a different receiver shape entirely and still needs this
    # explicit `.clone()`.
    req string? as_str():
        match self.clone():
            JStr(s): s
            _: nil

    req bool? as_bool():
        match self.clone():
            JBool(b): b
            _: nil

    req [JVal]? as_arr():
        match self.clone():
            JArr(a): a
            _: nil

    req int64? as_i64():
        match self.clone():
            JNum(n): n as int64
            _: nil

    # Render a JSON scalar (string/number/bool) as plain text, the way sb3
    # stores field/primitive values -- `nil` for null/array/object. Mirrors
    # the original Rust `json_value_to_display_string`. Numbers go through
    # ordinary float interpolation (`"{n}"`); Rust's own `f64` `Display`
    # already omits a trailing `.0` for an integral value (`25.0` -> "25"),
    # matching `serde_json::Number::to_string()`'s behavior for the common
    # case closely enough that no separate int/float distinction is kept.
    req string? as_display_string():
        match self.clone():
            JStr(s): s
            JNum(n): "{n}"
            JBool(b): "{b}"
            _: nil

# `items[idx]`'s display string, or `nil` if out of range or not a scalar --
# used throughout `parse_primitive`/`parse_field` below, same shape as the
# original Rust's own `get_str`/`arr.get(idx).and_then(...)` closures.
def string? jval_display_at([JVal] items, int idx):
    if idx < items.length:
        items[idx].as_display_string()
    else:
        nil

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw (pre-conversion) deserialization shapes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These mirror the on-disk JSON directly via `@derive(Deserialize)`. Field
# names are written in plain camelCase here specifically so they match sb3's
# real JSON keys verbatim -- Boring never renames a field the way it does a
# method name (see book.md's "Field names and JSON keys"), so no `@serde`
# annotation is needed anywhere in this section.

@derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)
struct RawProject:
    [RawTarget] targets
    @serde(default)
    [Sb3Monitor] monitors
    # `extensions`/`meta` intentionally not modeled -- serde ignores unknown
    # top-level fields by default, matching jscratch's own no-op parsing.

@derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)
struct RawTarget:
    bool isStage
    string name
    @serde(default)
    {string=[JVal]} variables
    @serde(default)
    {string=[JVal]} lists
    @serde(default)
    {string=string} broadcasts
    @serde(default)
    {string=RawBlock} blocks
    @serde(default)
    {string=Sb3Comment} comments
    @serde(default)
    int currentCostume
    @serde(default)
    [Sb3Costume] costumes
    @serde(default)
    [Sb3Sound] sounds
    @serde(default)
    float volume
    @serde(default)
    int layerOrder
    float? tempo
    float? videoTransparency
    string? videoState
    string? textToSpeechLanguage
    bool? visible
    float? x
    float? y
    float? size
    float? direction
    bool? draggable
    string? rotationStyle

@derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)
struct RawBlock:
    string opcode
    string? next
    string? parent
    @serde(default)
    {string=JVal} inputs
    @serde(default)
    {string=JVal} fields
    @serde(default)
    bool shadow
    @serde(default)
    bool topLevel
    float? x
    float? y
    RawMutation? mutation
    string? comment

# A block's `mutation` object. Real sb3 spells its own keys inconsistently
# (`tagName` is camelCase; `proccode`/`argumentids`/`argumentnames`/
# `argumentdefaults`/`warp`/`hasnext` are all-lowercase, no separators at
# all) -- field names below are spelled to match those exact keys verbatim,
# same "no rename annotation needed" reasoning as `RawTarget`/`RawBlock`
# above, just with uglier field names as the price. `argumentids`/
# `argumentnames`/`argumentdefaults` stay `string?` here (not `[string]?`)
# because they really are double-JSON-encoded on disk -- a JSON string that
# itself contains a JSON-encoded array -- decoded a second time by
# `decode_double_encoded_array` below, matching the original Rust's own
# "double-encoding, a real Scratch-format quirk" comment. `warp` is likewise
# kept as the raw string `"true"`/`"false"` here (not a `bool`) and converted
# in `convert_mutation`.
@derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)
struct RawMutation:
    string? tagName
    string? proccode
    string? argumentids
    string? argumentnames
    string? argumentdefaults
    string? warp
    string? hasnext
    # `children` (always an empty array in every real sb3 project observed)
    # is discarded by omission here, matching jscratch/the original Rust.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leaf structs -- deserialized directly, also the public/converted shape
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Unlike Target/Block/Variable/List/Mutation above, these four have no raw/
# converted split -- their own JSON shape is already fully static, so
# `@derive(Deserialize)` (plus a struct-level `@serde(rename_all =
# "camelCase")` so the Boring-side snake_case field names below still match
# sb3's camelCase JSON keys) is the whole conversion step, same as the
# original Rust. Snake_case field names (not camelCase, despite the rule
# above) are deliberate here: `boring/scratch.br` already reads
# `.data_format`/`.asset_id`/`.md5ext`/`.width`/`.height` directly as plain
# field accesses (confirmed by grep before writing this file) -- matching
# those exact names is required, `rename_all` is what reconciles that with
# the real camelCase JSON.

@derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)
@serde(rename_all = "camelCase")
pub struct Sb3Costume:
    string name
    string data_format
    string asset_id
    string md5ext
    float rotation_center_x
    float rotation_center_y
    @serde(default)
    float? bitmap_resolution

@derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)
@serde(rename_all = "camelCase")
pub struct Sb3Sound:
    string name
    string asset_id
    string data_format
    # Defaulted, not required -- a real scratch-vm fixture (`corrupt_png.sb3`)
    # has a sound entry with no `format` key at all, same robustness note as
    # the original Rust struct's own doc comment.
    @serde(default)
    string format
    float rate
    float sample_count
    string md5ext

@derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)
@serde(rename_all = "camelCase")
pub struct Sb3Comment:
    @serde(default)
    string? block_id
    float x
    float y
    float width
    float height
    @serde(default)
    bool minimized
    @serde(default)
    string text

# One entry in the project's top-level `monitors` array. `params`/`value`
# are `JVal` (see this file's own header comment) -- every real fixture
# observed uses plain strings/numbers there, but nothing in the format
# guarantees it. `width`/`height`/`sprite_name`/`slider_min`/`slider_max`/
# `is_discrete` are all defaulted -- confirmed necessary against real
# scratch-vm test fixtures that omit them (same reasoning as the original
# Rust struct's own doc comment).
@derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)
@serde(rename_all = "camelCase")
pub struct Sb3Monitor:
    string id
    string mode
    string opcode
    @serde(default)
    {string=JVal} params
    @serde(default)
    string? sprite_name
    @serde(default)
    JVal? value
    @serde(default)
    float width
    @serde(default)
    float height
    float x
    float y
    @serde(default)
    bool visible
    @serde(default)
    float? slider_min
    @serde(default)
    float? slider_max
    @serde(default)
    bool? is_discrete

    pub req bool is_variable_watcher():
        opcode == "data_variable"

    pub req bool is_list_watcher():
        opcode == "data_listcontents"

    pub req string variable_id():
        id

    pub req string? variable_name():
        if let v = params.get("VARIABLE"):
            v.as_str()
        else:
            nil

    pub req bool is_visible():
        visible

    pub req float32 x_pos():
        x as float32

    pub req float32 y_pos():
        y as float32

    pub req bool is_motion_xposition_watcher():
        opcode == "motion_xposition"

    pub req bool is_motion_yposition_watcher():
        opcode == "motion_yposition"

    pub req bool is_motion_direction_watcher():
        opcode == "motion_direction"

    pub req bool is_sensing_timer_watcher():
        opcode == "sensing_timer"

    pub req bool is_looks_size_watcher():
        opcode == "looks_size"

    pub req bool is_looks_costumenumbername_watcher():
        opcode == "looks_costumenumbername"

    # `NUMBER_NAME` -- "number" or "name" -- same field `looks_
    # costumenumbername`'s own block reads (`BlockKind::CostumeNumberName`'s
    # own doc comment), just resolved from a monitor's saved `params`
    # instead of a block's `fields` at link time. Falls back to "number",
    # real Scratch's own default when a monitor predates this field
    # existing at all (an older project format).
    pub req string number_name_param():
        if let v = params.get("NUMBER_NAME"):
            v.as_str() else "number"
        else:
            "number"

    pub req bool is_looks_backdropnumbername_watcher():
        opcode == "looks_backdropnumbername"

    pub req bool is_sound_volume_watcher():
        opcode == "sound_volume"

    pub req bool is_sensing_current_watcher():
        opcode == "sensing_current"

    pub req bool is_sensing_dayssince2000_watcher():
        opcode == "sensing_dayssince2000"

    pub req bool is_sensing_loudness_watcher():
        opcode == "sensing_loudness"

    # `CURRENTMENU` -- "YEAR"/"MONTH"/etc, same field `sensing_current`'s
    # own block reads (`BlockKind::CurrentDateTime`'s own doc comment),
    # resolved from a monitor's saved `params` instead of a block's
    # `fields`. Falls back to `""` (an unrecognized value, matching
    # `CurrentDateTime`'s own "unrecognized menu value reports 0" default)
    # rather than guessing a specific field -- unlike `NUMBER_NAME`, real
    # Scratch's own `current()` has no single "most common" default to
    # fall back to across all seven possible values.
    pub req string current_menu_param():
        if let v = params.get("CURRENTMENU"):
            v.as_str() else ""
        else:
            ""

    pub req string sprite_name_string():
        sprite_name else ""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public converted data model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Field names below are plain snake_case, matching the original Rust
# struct's own field names exactly -- `boring/scratch.br` already reads a
# good number of these directly (`.targets`, `.monitors`, `.variables`,
# `.lists`, `.current_costume`, `.costumes`, `.is_stage`, `.parent`, `.x`,
# `.y`, ...), confirmed by grep before writing this file. None of these
# derive `Deserialize` themselves -- they're built by `convert_target`/
# `parse_variable`/etc. below from the Raw* shapes above.

@derive(Debug, Clone)
pub struct Sb3Project:
    [Sb3Target] targets
    [Sb3Monitor] monitors

    # `Sb3Project.load_for_boring(...)`/`.load_json_for_boring(...)` (static,
    # `Type.method(args)` call syntax throughout `scratch.br`) need `type def`
    # here, not a plain top-level `def` -- a top-level function transpiles to
    # a free `fn`, never `impl Sb3Project { fn ... }`, so `Sb3Project::
    # load_for_boring(...)` wouldn't resolve at all (confirmed the hard way:
    # a real E0599 from `cargo build`, not a hypothetical). `pub type def`
    # (both keywords together) is what book.md's own "static factory method"
    # example uses for exactly this shape.

    # Load a `.sb3` project file (a zip archive containing `project.json`).
    # Collapses every failure to `nil` -- see this file's own header comment
    # on why there's no `Sb3LoadError` any more.
    pub type def Sb3Project? load_for_boring(string path):
        guard let contents = read_project_json_from_zip(path) else return nil
        project_from_json_text(contents)

    # Load a raw `project.json` file directly, with no surrounding zip
    # archive (used by hand-authored test fixtures, e.g.
    # `assets/procedures_nested.json` -- far easier to author directly than
    # a real `.sb3` zip). Boring's own built-in `fs.read` (book.md's "File
    # system" section), wrapped in `try?` per the docs' own documented idiom
    # for using it outside a `throws` function -- previously routed through
    # the external `std.fs.read_to_string` instead, worked around here
    # because `try? fs.read(...)` used to double-handle the already-`Option`-
    # producing plain form and fail to compile (docs/try-wrap-double-
    # handling-bug.md in the `boring` repo, fixed upstream).
    pub type def Sb3Project? load_json_for_boring(string path):
        let contentsOpt = try? fs.read(path)
        guard let contents = contentsOpt else return nil
        project_from_json_text("{contents}")

@derive(Debug, Clone)
pub struct Sb3Target:
    bool is_stage
    string name
    [Sb3Variable] variables
    [Sb3List] lists
    [Sb3Broadcast] broadcasts
    {string=Sb3Block} blocks
    {string=Sb3Comment} comments
    int current_costume
    [Sb3Costume] costumes
    [Sb3Sound] sounds
    float volume
    int layer_order

    # Stage-only fields (nil on sprites).
    float? tempo
    float? video_transparency
    string? video_state
    string? text_to_speech_language

    # Sprite-only fields (nil on the Stage).
    bool? visible
    float? x
    float? y
    float? size
    float? direction
    bool? draggable
    string? rotation_style

    pub req Sb3Block? get_block(string id):
        blocks.get(id)

    # A plain 2-variable `for id, b in blocks:` destructuring loop over a
    # bare `{K=V}` dict field (the `id` is unused in either method) used to
    # mis-transpile to `self.blocks.clone().into_iter().enumerate().map(|(i,
    # v)| (i as isize, v))`, treating the dict as if it needed array-style
    # auto-enumeration (`b` ended up bound to the whole `(id, b)` tuple, not
    # `Sb3Block` -- a real E0609 "no field `top_level`/`opcode` on
    # `(Arc<str>, Sb3Block)`" from `cargo build`). Fixed upstream
    # (docs/self-field-loop-match-borrow-bug.md in the `boring` repo) -- this
    # used to route through `blocks.values()` instead, to sidestep the
    # destructuring entirely.
    pub req Sb3Block? find_top_level_hat(string opcode):
        for id, b in blocks:
            if b.top_level and b.opcode == opcode:
                return b
        nil

    pub req [Sb3Block] all_blocks():
        mut out = []
        for id, b in blocks:
            out.push(b)
        out

    pub req float32 x_or(float32 default):
        if let v = x:
            v as float32
        else:
            default

    pub req float32 y_or(float32 default):
        if let v = y:
            v as float32
        else:
            default

    pub req float32 direction_or(float32 default):
        if let v = direction:
            v as float32
        else:
            default

    pub req float32 size_or(float32 default):
        if let v = size:
            v as float32
        else:
            default

    pub req bool visible_or(bool default):
        if let v = visible:
            v
        else:
            default

    # Real Scratch's own default when a target's `rotationStyle` is
    # absent (the Stage never has one at all -- it can't rotate -- and a
    # hand-authored raw-JSON fixture may omit it too) is "all around",
    # confirmed against real scratch-vm (`RenderedTarget`'s own
    # `this.rotationStyle = RenderedTarget.ROTATION_STYLE_ALL_AROUND`
    # constructor default).
    pub req string rotation_style_or_default():
        rotation_style else "all around"

    pub req string name_string():
        name

    pub req bool name_matches(string other):
        name == other

    pub req Sb3Sound? sound_by_name(string soundName):
        for s in sounds:
            if s.name == soundName:
                return s
        nil

# `variables: {"<id>": ["<name>", <initial_value>]}`, or the 3-element
# cloud-variable form `["<name>", <initial_value>, true]`.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
pub struct Sb3Variable:
    string id
    string name
    JVal value
    bool cloud

    pub req string? initial_as_number_string():
        match value:
            JNum(n): "{n}"
            _: nil

    pub req string? initial_as_text():
        match value:
            JStr(s): s
            _: nil

    pub req bool? initial_as_bool():
        match value:
            JBool(b): b
            _: nil

    pub req bool name_matches(string other):
        name == other

    pub req string id_string():
        id

    pub req string name_string():
        name

# `lists: {"<id>": ["<name>", [<items>...]]}`.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
pub struct Sb3List:
    string id
    string name
    [JVal] items

    pub req string id_string():
        id

    pub req bool name_matches(string other):
        name == other

    pub req string name_string():
        name

    pub req int item_count():
        items.length

    pub req string? item_as_number_string(int i):
        if i >= 0 and i < items.length:
            match items[i].clone():
                JNum(n): "{n}"
                _: nil
        else:
            nil

    pub req string? item_as_text(int i):
        if i >= 0 and i < items.length:
            match items[i].clone():
                JStr(s): s
                _: nil
        else:
            nil

    pub req bool? item_as_bool(int i):
        if i >= 0 and i < items.length:
            match items[i].clone():
                JBool(b): b
                _: nil
        else:
            nil

# `broadcasts: {"<id>": "<name>"}`. Never read directly by `scratch.br`
# today (broadcasts are resolved by name via block fields instead, confirmed
# by grep) -- kept for API completeness/fidelity with the original loader.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
pub struct Sb3Broadcast:
    string id
    string name

# The resolved value of a block input: either a reference to another block
# (by id, looked up in the same target's `blocks` map), or an inline
# primitive value. Never pattern-matched from `scratch.br` directly
# (confirmed by grep) -- only through `Sb3Block`'s own `input_block_id`/
# `input_primitive` glue methods below, so this stays non-`pub`.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
enum Sb3InputValue:
    Block(string)
    Primitive(Sb3Primitive)

# One entry of a block's `inputs` map. `shadow_fallback` is the "obscured
# shadow" value underneath a real block/primitive when the input's shadow
# status is `3` -- see the original Rust struct's own doc comment for why
# it's kept at all. Non-`pub`, same reasoning as `Sb3InputValue`.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
struct Sb3Input:
    Sb3InputValue value
    Sb3Primitive? shadow_fallback

# An inline "primitive" value that can appear as a block input, per the sb3
# spec's primitive codes 4..=13. `pub` -- `scratch.br`'s `resolve_primitive`
# takes this directly as a parameter.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
pub enum Sb3Primitive:
    Number(string)
    Text(string)
    Broadcast(string name, string id)
    Variable(string name, string id)
    List(string name, string id)
    ColorPicker(string)

    pub req string? as_number_str():
        match self.clone():
            Number(s): s
            _: nil

    pub req string? as_text():
        match self.clone():
            Text(s): s
            _: nil

    pub req string? as_variable_id():
        match self.clone():
            Variable(name, id): id
            _: nil

    pub req string? as_broadcast_name():
        match self.clone():
            Broadcast(name, id): name
            _: nil

# `fields: {"<FIELD_NAME>": ["<value>", "<id_or_null>"]}`. Never read
# directly by `scratch.br` (confirmed by grep) -- only through `Sb3Block`'s
# `field_value`/`field_id` glue methods, so this stays non-`pub`.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
struct Sb3Field:
    string value
    string? id

# A block's `mutation` object, converted from `RawMutation`. Non-`pub` --
# only reachable via `Sb3Block`'s `mutation_*` glue methods.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
struct Sb3Mutation:
    string tag_name
    string? proc_code
    [string]? argument_ids
    [string]? argument_names
    [string]? argument_defaults
    bool? warp
    string? has_next

# One block in a target's `blocks` map. `pub` -- passed directly as a
# parameter/return type all over `scratch.br`'s linker.
@derive(Debug, Clone)
pub struct Sb3Block:
    string opcode
    string? next
    string? parent
    {string=Sb3Input} inputs
    {string=Sb3Field} fields
    bool shadow
    bool top_level
    float? x
    float? y
    Sb3Mutation? mutation
    string? comment

    pub req string? next_id():
        next

    pub req string? input_block_id(string name):
        if let inp = inputs.get(name):
            match inp.value.clone():
                Block(id): id
                _: nil
        else:
            nil

    pub req Sb3Primitive? input_primitive(string name):
        if let inp = inputs.get(name):
            match inp.value.clone():
                Primitive(p): p
                _: nil
        else:
            nil

    pub req string? field_value(string name):
        if let f = fields.get(name):
            f.value
        else:
            nil

    pub req string? field_id(string name):
        if let f = fields.get(name):
            f.id
        else:
            nil

    pub req bool opcode_is(string op):
        opcode == op

    pub req string? mutation_proc_code():
        if let m = mutation:
            m.proc_code
        else:
            nil

    pub req [string] mutation_argument_ids():
        if let m = mutation:
            m.argument_ids else []
        else:
            []

    pub req [string] mutation_argument_names():
        if let m = mutation:
            m.argument_names else []
        else:
            []

    # A `procedures_prototype`'s own "Run without screen refresh" flag
    # (real Scratch's own "warp" -- confirmed against source, e.g.
    # `Scratch3ProcedureBlocks`/`Sequencer.stepThread`'s `thread.warp`
    # check). Absent (an old/malformed project, or any non-prototype
    # block) defaults to `false` -- the safe, ordinary-yielding default,
    # same "missing metadata degrades to the non-special-cased behavior"
    # rule `mutation_argument_names`' own `else []` already follows.
    pub req bool mutation_warp():
        if let m = mutation:
            m.warp else false
        else:
            false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw -> public struct conversion
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def Sb3Variable? parse_variable(string id, [JVal] arr):
    guard arr.length >= 2 else return nil
    guard let name = arr[0].as_str() else return nil
    let value = arr[1]
    var cloud = false
    if arr.length >= 3:
        if let c = arr[2].as_bool():
            cloud = c
    Sb3Variable(id = id, name = name, value = value, cloud = cloud)

def Sb3List? parse_list(string id, [JVal] arr):
    guard arr.length >= 2 else return nil
    guard let name = arr[0].as_str() else return nil
    guard let items = arr[1].as_arr() else return nil
    Sb3List(id = id, name = name, items = items)

# Parse a primitive array `[code, ...]` per the sb3 primitive codes. Codes
# 4-8 are all "just a numeric literal string" as far as runtime semantics
# go -- the distinction between them is an editor input-validation hint, not
# a runtime type -- so they're all stored uniformly as `Number`, matching
# the original Rust's own reasoning.
def Sb3Primitive? parse_primitive(JVal raw):
    guard let items = raw.as_arr() else return nil
    guard items.length >= 1 else return nil
    guard let code = items[0].as_i64() else return nil
    match code:
        4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8:
            guard let s = jval_display_at(items, 1) else return nil
            Sb3Primitive.Number(s)
        10:
            guard let s = jval_display_at(items, 1) else return nil
            Sb3Primitive.Text(s)
        9:
            # Known gap (also left unimplemented by jscratch): the
            # colour_picker literal is kept as an opaque string, no color
            # parsing attempted -- matching the original Rust's own comment.
            guard let s = jval_display_at(items, 1) else return nil
            Sb3Primitive.ColorPicker(s)
        11:
            guard let name = jval_display_at(items, 1) else return nil
            let id = jval_display_at(items, 2) else ""
            Sb3Primitive.Broadcast(name, id)
        12:
            guard let name = jval_display_at(items, 1) else return nil
            let id = jval_display_at(items, 2) else ""
            Sb3Primitive.Variable(name, id)
        13:
            guard let name = jval_display_at(items, 1) else return nil
            let id = jval_display_at(items, 2) else ""
            Sb3Primitive.List(name, id)
        _: nil

# The second element of an input array is either a block id (string, looked
# up in the target's `blocks` map) or an inline primitive array.
def Sb3InputValue? parse_input_value(JVal raw):
    match raw:
        JStr(s): Sb3InputValue.Block(s)
        JArr(_):
            guard let p = parse_primitive(raw) else return nil
            Sb3InputValue.Primitive(p)
        _: nil

# Parse one `inputs` entry: `[shadow_status, value_or_id, shadow_fallback?]`.
def Sb3Input? parse_input(JVal raw):
    guard let items = raw.as_arr() else return nil
    guard items.length >= 2 else return nil
    guard let shadow_status = items[0].as_i64() else return nil
    guard let value = parse_input_value(items[1]) else return nil
    match shadow_status:
        # 1 = unobscured shadow, 2 = no shadow: no third element to consider.
        1 | 2:
            Sb3Input(value = value, shadow_fallback = nil)
        # 3 = obscured shadow: a real value/block, with a shadow fallback
        # underneath as the third array element.
        3:
            var Sb3Primitive? fallback = nil
            if items.length >= 3:
                fallback = parse_primitive(items[2])
            Sb3Input(value = value, shadow_fallback = fallback)
        _: nil

# Parse one `fields` entry: `["<value>", "<id_or_null>"]`.
def Sb3Field? parse_field(JVal raw):
    guard let items = raw.as_arr() else return nil
    guard let value = jval_display_at(items, 0) else return nil
    var string? id = nil
    if items.length >= 2:
        id = items[1].as_str()
    Sb3Field(value = value, id = id)

# `argumentids`/`argumentnames`/`argumentdefaults` are stored as a JSON
# string that itself contains a JSON-encoded array (double-encoding, a real
# Scratch-format quirk) -- `fromJson` undoes the inner encoding directly,
# no manual object-walking needed (unlike the original Rust, which had to
# hand-decode this from a raw `serde_json::Value` object). Wrapped in `try?`
# per book.md's documented idiom outside a `throws` function (docs/try-wrap-
# double-handling-bug.md in the `boring` repo used to make that combination
# fail to compile; fixed upstream, this used to be a bare `fromJson<...>(s)`
# call as a workaround).
def [string]? decode_double_encoded_array(string? raw):
    guard let s = raw else return nil
    # Parens required: `guard let`'s clause parser calls into the expression
    # grammar one precedence level below where the bare `try`/`try?` prefix is
    # recognized (same reason tests/cases/try_else_nil_if_let.br's own
    # `if let r = (try? risky(v)):` needs them, in the `boring` repo).
    guard let arr = (try? fromJson<[string]>(s)) else return nil
    arr

# Parse a block's `mutation` object. Trivial compared to the original Rust
# version: `RawMutation` already gave us typed fields directly via serde, so
# this is just normalizing `tagName`'s default and `warp`'s string-to-bool
# conversion, plus double-decoding the three argument lists.
def Sb3Mutation convert_mutation(RawMutation rm):
    let tag_name = rm.tagName else "mutation"
    var bool? warp = nil
    if let w = rm.warp:
        warp = (w == "true")
    Sb3Mutation(
        tag_name = tag_name,
        proc_code = rm.proccode,
        argument_ids = decode_double_encoded_array(rm.argumentids),
        argument_names = decode_double_encoded_array(rm.argumentnames),
        argument_defaults = decode_double_encoded_array(rm.argumentdefaults),
        warp = warp,
        has_next = rm.hasnext,
    )

def Sb3Block convert_block(RawBlock rb):
    mut inputs = {=}
    for name, raw in rb.inputs:
        if let parsed = parse_input(raw):
            inputs[name] = parsed
    mut fields = {=}
    for name, raw in rb.fields:
        if let parsed = parse_field(raw):
            fields[name] = parsed
    var Sb3Mutation? mutation = nil
    if let rm = rb.mutation:
        mutation = convert_mutation(rm)
    Sb3Block(
        opcode = rb.opcode,
        next = rb.next,
        parent = rb.parent,
        inputs = inputs,
        fields = fields,
        shadow = rb.shadow,
        top_level = rb.topLevel,
        x = rb.x,
        y = rb.y,
        mutation = mutation,
        comment = rb.comment,
    )

def Sb3Target convert_target(RawTarget rt):
    mut variables = []
    for id, arr in rt.variables:
        if let v = parse_variable(id, arr):
            variables.push(v)
    mut lists = []
    for id, arr in rt.lists:
        if let l = parse_list(id, arr):
            lists.push(l)
    mut broadcasts = []
    for id, name in rt.broadcasts:
        broadcasts.push(Sb3Broadcast(id = id, name = name))
    mut blocks = {=}
    for id, rb in rt.blocks:
        blocks[id] = convert_block(rb)
    Sb3Target(
        is_stage = rt.isStage,
        name = rt.name,
        variables = variables,
        lists = lists,
        broadcasts = broadcasts,
        blocks = blocks,
        comments = rt.comments,
        current_costume = rt.currentCostume,
        costumes = rt.costumes,
        sounds = rt.sounds,
        volume = rt.volume,
        layer_order = rt.layerOrder,
        tempo = rt.tempo,
        video_transparency = rt.videoTransparency,
        video_state = rt.videoState,
        text_to_speech_language = rt.textToSpeechLanguage,
        visible = rt.visible,
        x = rt.x,
        y = rt.y,
        size = rt.size,
        direction = rt.direction,
        draggable = rt.draggable,
        rotation_style = rt.rotationStyle,
    )

# How deep a `project.json`'s own `{`/`[` nesting is allowed to go before
# `json_nesting_within_limit` (below) rejects it outright. Generous on
# purpose -- a real project.json only ever nests project -> targets ->
# block -> inputs/fields -> primitive array, a handful of levels -- so
# this never trips on legitimate content.
let int MAX_JSON_NESTING_DEPTH = 256

# Scans `text` (the raw `project.json` source, *before* any real parsing)
# for its maximum brace/bracket nesting depth, `false` as soon as it goes
# past `max_depth` -- deliberately not a real JSON tokenizer (no number/
# keyword handling), just enough to track where a quoted string starts/
# ends (so a `{`/`[` inside string *content* is never mistaken for real
# structure) and count depth outside of one. `JVal` (this file's own
# dynamic-JSON enum, used for every block's `inputs`/`fields`, a
# variable's initial value, etc. -- see this file's own header comment)
# is exactly as deeply recursive as whatever JSON it's asked to parse:
# with no limit at all, a hand-forged `project.json` containing a value
# nested tens of thousands of levels deep (e.g. a block input shaped like
# `[[[[[...]]]]]`) overflows the stack *during deserialization itself* --
# before `fromJson::<RawProject>` below even returns a result to check --
# independent of every other cycle/depth guard this project has
# elsewhere (`linker.br`'s own `resolve_expr_block`/`resolve_stack`,
# which only ever run *after* a project has already finished loading).
def bool json_nesting_within_limit(string text, int max_depth):
    var mut depth = 0
    var mut in_string = false
    var mut escaped = false
    mut chars = text.chars()
    for c in chars:
        let s = "{c}"
        if in_string:
            if escaped:
                escaped = false
            elif s == "\\":
                escaped = true
            elif s == "\"":
                in_string = false
        else:
            if s == "\"":
                in_string = true
            elif s == "{{" or s == "[":
                depth = depth + 1
                if depth > max_depth:
                    return false
            elif s == "}}" or s == "]":
                depth = depth - 1
    true

def Sb3Project? project_from_json_text(string contents):
    if !json_nesting_within_limit(contents.clone(), MAX_JSON_NESTING_DEPTH):
        return nil
    let rawOpt = try? fromJson<RawProject>(contents)
    guard let raw = rawOpt else return nil
    mut targets = []
    for rt in raw.targets:
        targets.push(convert_target(rt))
    Sb3Project(targets = targets, monitors = raw.monitors)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Loading entry points
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both entry points collapse every failure to `nil` -- matching the original
# file's own Boring-integration section, which already did this for the
# exact same reason (`Result<_, Sb3LoadError>` isn't Boring's own `throws`
# machinery). This is now the *only* loading API (the original's richer
# `Result`-returning `Sb3Project::load`/`load_json` are gone, along with
# `Sb3LoadError` itself) -- `tests/scratch_vm_fixtures.rs`'s two direct
# Rust-side callers were updated to use `load_for_boring` instead.

# `name` matches `filename` either exactly (entry at the zip root) or as
# `<single-path-segment>/filename` (one directory level deep) -- mirrors
# real Scratch's own loading step (`scratch-parser`'s `lib/unzip.js`,
# `^([^/]*\/)?project\.json$`), which accepts a `.sb3` that's really a
# zipped *folder* (e.g. produced by "Compress" on a folder in a file
# manager, not exported by the Scratch editor itself). Confirmed a real
# shape via real scratch-vm test fixtures (`missing_svg.sb3`/
# `corrupt_svg.sb3`), same as the original Rust's own comment. Splitting on
# `/` and counting segments is this file's own equivalent of the original's
# `prefix.ends_with('/') && !prefix[..len-1].contains('/')` check: exactly
# one segment means "at the root"; exactly two means "one directory deep";
# three or more means "nested too deep, reject".
def bool zip_entry_matches(string name, string filename):
    let parts = name.split("/")
    if parts.length == 1:
        parts[0] == filename
    elif parts.length == 2:
        parts[1] == filename
    else:
        false

# Shared cap on a single zip entry's *declared* uncompressed size --
# checked (via `ZipFile.size()`, the entry's own central-directory field)
# before either `read_project_json_from_zip` below or `assets.br`'s
# `read_zip_entry_bytes` calls `ZipFile.readToEnd`. Both extract a single
# entry out of an attacker-controlled `.sb3` into an in-memory buffer with
# no size limit at all otherwise -- a "zip bomb" (a tiny file, extreme
# compression ratio) declares a genuinely enormous uncompressed size for
# one entry, and `read_to_end` happily keeps growing its buffer to match,
# allocating gigabytes from a download of a few KB, before a single byte
# of that entry is ever used. 100 MiB is comfortably above any real
# project.json or any individual costume/sound asset a real `.sb3` ever
# contains (assets are typically KB-to-low-MB), so this never trips on
# legitimate content. One shared constant (not duplicated per-file) since
# `sb3_loader.br`/`assets.br` are sibling files spliced into the same flat
# generated-Rust scope (see this file's own header comment) -- a second,
# differently-valued definition of the same limit in each file would be
# easy to let drift out of sync.
let uint64 MAX_ZIP_ENTRY_BYTES = 104857600 as uint64

# Opens `sb3Path` as a zip archive and returns `project.json`'s full text
# (wherever it sits per `zip_entry_matches` above), or `nil` on any I/O/zip
# error or a missing entry, or an entry whose declared size exceeds
# `MAX_ZIP_ENTRY_BYTES` (see that constant's own doc comment).
def string? read_project_json_from_zip(string sb3Path):
    let fileOpt = try? File.open(sb3Path.asRef())
    guard let file = fileOpt else return nil
    let archiveOpt = try? ZipArchive.new(file)
    guard let archiveFixed = archiveOpt else return nil
    var mut archive = archiveFixed
    let entryCount = ZipArchive.len(archive)
    var string? foundName = nil
    var mut i = 0 as uint
    while i < entryCount:
        let indexedOpt = try? ZipArchive.byIndex(archive, i)
        if let entry = indexedOpt:
            let rawName = entry.name()
            let nameStr = "{rawName}"
            if zip_entry_matches(nameStr.clone(), "project.json" as string):
                foundName = nameStr
        i = i + (1 as uint)
    guard let entryName = foundName else return nil
    let entryOpt = try? ZipArchive.byName(archive, entryName)
    guard let entryFixed = entryOpt else return nil
    var mut entry = entryFixed
    if entry.size() > MAX_ZIP_ENTRY_BYTES:
        return nil
    var [uint8] buf = []
    let bytesReadOpt = try? ZipFile.readToEnd(entry, buf)
    guard let bytesRead = bytesReadOpt else return nil
    let contentsOpt = try? String.fromUtf8(buf)
    guard let contents = contentsOpt else return nil
    # `String::from_utf8` returns a real Rust `String`, not Boring's own
    # `Arc<str>` string representation -- interpolation is what converts it
    # (matches `zip_entry_matches`' own `"{rawName}"` conversion of a
    # borrowed `&str` earlier in this same function).
    "{contents}"

